data.content:
I have the answer from the reference docs.

## What happened

In C# 14 (.NET 10), **new span conversions** change overload resolution. Your `array.Contains(num)` in an expression tree now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

Expression trees **cannot represent calls to span-based methods**, so this throws at runtime (typically `NotSupportedException` or `InvalidOperationException`).

## How to fix it

Force the compiler to pick the `Enumerable` overload by casting to `IEnumerable<T>` or using `.AsEnumerable()`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevents the span overload from being selected and keeps the expression tree representable.
